PR #2607  overall: potential_risk
Spec: `mtval` is virtual-address-only when paging is on (even for physical access faults); treating it as a physical address in trap handlers leaks virtual address-space layout and produces incorrect fault attribution
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/entry.S:125-194  handle_exception
      Unconditionally saves CSR_TVAL (mtval/stval) to PT_BADADDR for ALL exception types including EXC_LOAD_ACCESS/EXC_STORE_ACCESS/EXC_INST_ACCESS (PMP violations). When paging is enabled, the RISC-V spec mandates mtval carries the faulting VA even for physical access faults. The field is named 'badaddr' with no VA/PA qualifier, and no metadata (e.g., satp.MODE) is saved alongside to allow downstream consumers to distinguish address-space semantics. Any consumer reading regs->badaddr for an access fault must independently know whether paging was enabled to interpret the value correctly — this invariant is not enforced in code.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/traps.c:198-199  do_trap_load_fault
      Handler for EXC_LOAD_ACCESS (cause=5) — the designated consumption point for mtval on load PMP violations. The DO_ERROR_INFO macro passes regs->epc (instruction PC) as the fault address rather than regs->badaddr. This accidentally side-steps the VA/PA confusion because badaddr (which holds mtval) is never consumed. However, the actual faulting data address in badaddr is silently discarded — any future extension to report the faulting data address via badaddr would introduce the VA-as-PA type confusion, since for a PMP violation with paging enabled, badaddr holds a VA, not the offending PA.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/traps.c:252-253  do_trap_store_fault
      Handler for EXC_STORE_ACCESS (cause=7) — same pattern as do_trap_load_fault. DO_ERROR_INFO passes regs->epc as the fault address, ignoring regs->badaddr entirely. badaddr is available in pt_regs but unused. This handler is the natural place where an M-mode kernel or future extension would consume mtval for physical-access-fault diagnosis, but any such use would need to check satp.MODE before interpreting badaddr as PA vs VA. No such guard exists.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/traps.c:170-171  do_trap_insn_fault
      Handler for EXC_INST_ACCESS (cause=1). DO_ERROR_INFO passes regs->epc as the fault address. For instruction access faults, mtval contains the faulting instruction address, which equals regs->epc regardless of whether paging is enabled (both are VA if paging on, both are PA if paging off). The handler is accidentally correct in both cases, and badaddr is not consumed — no VA/PA confusion risk for this specific exception type.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/traps.c:132-143  do_trap_error
      Common backend for all access-fault handlers. Stores regs->cause into thread.bad_cause but receives 'addr' (always regs->epc from all current callers) and passes it to die()/do_trap(). The function signature takes a generic 'unsigned long addr' parameter — there is no type annotation indicating whether this is a VA or PA. If any future caller passes regs->badaddr as the addr argument (e.g., to report the faulting data address for load/store access faults), the VA-as-PA confusion would propagate to crash diagnostics via die(). No guard exists to prevent this.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/process.c:99-100  __show_regs
      Prints regs->badaddr unconditionally with the label 'badaddr' in crash/debug register dumps, without checking scause or satp.MODE. For a PMP violation with paging enabled, this prints the faulting VA under a label that strongly suggests a physical address in the access-fault context. A developer or automated crash-analysis tool reading 'badaddr' in an access-fault crash log could misinterpret the value as the offending physical address, leading to incorrect fault diagnosis and potentially leaking virtual-address layout information through crash logs.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/mm/fault.c:278-289  handle_page_fault
      Reads regs->badaddr as the faulting VA and passes it through the full VM subsystem (show_pte, lock_vma_under_rcu, handle_mm_fault). Currently only reached for page-fault causes (12/13/15), so VA interpretation is correct per spec. However, the code implicitly trusts badaddr is always a VA — this assumption is not checked locally (no assertion on scause range). If access faults (causes 1/5/7, PMP violations) were ever routed here, the same field name would carry different address-space semantics depending on cause and satp.MODE, breaking the VM logic.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/entry.S:38-41  handle_exception
