      Writes trap->stval into CSR_VSTVAL for guest delivery. For redirected EXC_LOAD_ACCESS/EXC_STORE_ACCESS (PMP violations), the guest receives whatever the host stval/mtval held — which is a VA when host paging is enabled. The normative rule (stval is always VA when paging enabled, even for physical access faults) validates this forwarding is semantically correct: the guest always receives a VA in vstval, which is what the guest's trap handler expects. The consistency is maintained across the virtualization boundary.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/traps.c:374-398  handle_user_cfi_violation
      Reads CSR_TVAL to check for CFI_TVAL_FCFI_CODE (2) or CFI_TVAL_BCFI_CODE (3) — hardware-defined exception codes for Zicfilp/Zicfiss violations (cause=18, software check exception). Correctly interprets tval as a code value rather than an address. This is the proper pattern: the interpretation of tval depends on scause, and this handler is only invoked for cause=18 where tval carries a code per the CFI spec. Demonstrates that tval's semantics are cause-dependent and code must check scause before interpreting.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/head.S:222-242  _start_kernel
      M-mode boot PMP initialization using a fault-tolerant pattern: installs .Lpmp_done as the trap handler, then attempts PMP CSR writes. If PMPs are unimplemented, the CSR access traps; the handler at .Lpmp_done dismisses the trap without examining mtval. At this boot stage, paging is not yet enabled (satp.MODE=bare), so mtval on the illegal-instruction trap would contain the physical address of the CSR-access instruction — but the handler doesn't read mtval at all, so no confusion is possible. The pattern is mitigation-aware: it avoids interpreting mtval entirely rather than making assumptions about its content.
  [OK] arch/riscv/errata/sifive/errata_cip_453.S:34-38  sifive_cip_453_insn_fault_trp
      Erratum workaround: reads PT_BADADDR from the trap frame and sign-extends bit 38 to correct a hardware bug where insn-fault mtval lacks proper sign extension. For EXC_INST_ACCESS faults, mtval (and therefore badaddr) contains the faulting instruction address — which per the normative rule is a VA when paging is enabled. The assumption that badaddr contains a VA for instruction faults is correct. The code modifies badaddr in-place (sign-extending), then dispatches to do_trap_insn_fault which passes regs->epc (not badaddr), so the sign-extended badaddr is never consumed by the current handler — the fix is benign but structurally sound.
